diagnosis_text:
A thorough end-to-end investigation of every layer from PC1 out to the ISP found no network fault. PC1 (10.1.0.100/24) reaches its Layer 3 gateway on dswa1 (10.1.0.1) with sub-millisecond latency, traverses csw1/csw2 to r1 via a healthy OSPF-learned default route, and exits through r1's NAT (MASQUERADE on eth2 toward ISPA) to reach all simulated internet destinations with 0% packet loss and ~1.3 ms round-trip time. The DNS server at 10.5.0.4 is equally reachable with 0% loss. No traffic shaping, rate limiting, or delay injection (netem/tbf/htb) exists on any interface across PC1, aswa2, dswa1, dswa2, csw1, csw2, r1, or ISPA — every device shows only the default `fq_codel` qdisc. There is no duplex mismatch on the PC1–aswa2 link (confirmed 10 Gbps full-duplex). The STP BLOCKING state on aswa2.eth2 is the correct, stable port-role for loop prevention in a dual-uplink design (dswa1 is root at priority 4096; TCN timer is zero). OSPF has been fully converged for over 15 minutes with no neighbor flaps. The MAC forwarding database on dswa2 correctly points PC1's MAC to bond0 (not the blocked eth1 port), so the ECMP path through dswa2 works without loss via the inter-switch bond. The user's report of "slow, intermittent internet from PC1" does not correspond to any active network fault; the infrastructure is healthy, and the perceived issue is non-network in origin (client-side problem, browser, or endpoint resource contention).
